the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186151994 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 false] [17592186151995 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186151995 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 false] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431538 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431539 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431541 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431533 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431534 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431531 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431537 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431540 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431532 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431535 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431536 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152360 1050 1020.0 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152401 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152401 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 2y = 0, y(0) = 1, y^{′}(0) =-4$." 13194139534661 17592186045417 false] [17592186152401 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+2B{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+2B=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -A+2B=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152402 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152402 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 0, y(0) =-2, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139534661 17592186045417 false] [17592186152402 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda =-3,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=-2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+B=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=-2$ gives $B=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152777 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186152777 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 false] [17592186153337 1064 "Let's find the particular solution for the **initial value problem**:\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' + 6y =0, \\ \\ y(0) = 1, \\ \\ y' (0) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe start with the general solution:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Apply the first initial condition, $y(0)=1.$**\nSubstituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution gives our first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Apply the second initial condition, $y'(0)=1.$**\nFirst, we need to find the derivative, $y'(x){:}$\n\n$$\ny'(x) =  -2 A e ^ { -2 x}  -3 B e ^ {-3 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y'=1$ gives our second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =-2A{e}^{0}-3B{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =-2A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve the system of equations for $A$ and $B.$**\nWe have a system of two linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ 1=-2A-3B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we can write $A=1-B.$ Substituting this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2(1-B)-3B \\\\ 1 & =-2+2B-3B \\\\ 3 & =-B \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, find $A{:}$ \n\n$$\nA = 1-B = 1 - (-3) = 4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Write the particular solution.**\nSubstituting $A=4$ and $B=-3$ back into the general solution gives the unique solution to the IVP:\n\n$$\ny(x) = 4e^{-2x} - 3 e^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139534661 17592186045417 true] [17592186153337 1064 "Let's find the particular solution for the **initial value problem**:\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' + 6y =0